Bootstrap: docker
From: node:22-slim

%post
    set -e
    npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code
    claude --version

%environment
    # ~/.conda/envs/r-to-python lives under $HOME, which this cluster's
    # apptainer.conf binds automatically (`mount home = yes`), so the env's
    # own bin/ is reachable without needing the host conda installation
    # (that lives under /software, which is NOT auto-bound).
    export CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV=r-to-python
    export CONDA_PREFIX="$HOME/.conda/envs/r-to-python"
    export PATH="$HOME/.conda/envs/r-to-python/bin:$PATH"

%test
    claude --version

%help
    Headless Claude Code CLI for the r2py ablation SGE jobs, with the
    r-to-python conda env active on PATH by default.

    Build (no admin/subuid setup needed on this cluster -- verified: builds
    fall back to a root-mapped namespace automatically):
        apptainer build --fakeroot claude_env.img claude_env.def

    Run:
        apptainer exec --bind /groups claude_env.img \
            claude -p "<prompt>" --dangerously-skip-permissions

    Notes:
      - $HOME is bound automatically; anything under ~ (the conda env,
        ~/.claude config, etc.) is visible with no extra flags.
      - /groups is NOT auto-bound on this cluster (mount hostfs = no,
        no bind path entry) -- verified empirically -- so it must be
        passed via --bind explicitly, as above, for the container to see
        this repository.
      - If something inside a job needs the real `conda` command (not just
        the r-to-python env's own binaries on PATH), also bind /software,
        e.g. --bind /groups,/software, since conda's base install lives
        there rather than under $HOME.
